\section{Search Protocol and Screening}
\label{app:search}

\paragraph{Identification.} We queried the DataCite REST API, which registers a DOI
(10.48550/arXiv.\textit{id}) for every arXiv paper, restricted to the client \texttt{arxiv.content},
with the query \texttt{(titles.title:sycophan* OR descriptions.description:sycophan*)}, a prefix match
on titles and abstracts. The query ran on 29 October 2026 (01:47 UTC) and returned 523 records; the
423 registered on or before 20 August 2026, the submission date, form the screened set, so later
work cannot enter it. Figure~\ref{fig:searchflow} gives the flow.

\paragraph{Screening.} A record was retained when AI sycophancy is a primary subject: the title
names it, or the abstract states that measuring, explaining, mitigating, or analyzing the
consequences of sycophancy is a main contribution or finding. Exclusion codes are passing mention,
non-AI sycophancy, and other. Each retained record received one role (benchmark, mitigation,
mechanism, measurement without a reusable instrument, human effects, survey or position, other)
and flags for covert forms, displacement, adaptive adversaries, third-party content, and
multi-turn pressure. The first pass was LLM-assisted under this fixed written protocol, in four
parallel batches; every decision carries a verbatim title or abstract quote, checked by script to
be an exact substring. We then hand-adjudicated every benchmark record (dedicated versus
multi-construct instrument), every mitigation absent from the submitted Table~\ref{tab:mitigation_comparison},
and every flagged record that bears on a claim in this paper, reading full texts where the abstract
did not settle the question.

\paragraph{Instrument coding.} An instrument is dedicated when sycophancy, or conformity to user
pressure, is its primary or co-primary target; 43 of the 60 retained benchmark records qualify, and
Pressure-Tune, retained under the mitigation role, makes 44. Each is coded by the reference against
which a response is judged sycophantic (R, C, N, E; Section~\ref{subsec:reftypes}).

\paragraph{Recall and limitations.} The query returns 33 of the 49 arXiv papers in the snowball
corpus; the 16 it misses are background or adjacent-construct papers (RLHF, reward hacking,
steering, faithfulness, prompt injection) whose titles and abstracts, as registered with DataCite,
never use the term. Identification is arXiv-only, so venue-only work is covered by the snowball
corpus rather than the query, and coding is at title and abstract level except where noted.

