\section{Measurement Metrics Catalog}
\label{app:metrics}

Table~\ref{tab:metrics} catalogs the primary metrics used across the evaluation literature. The
methodological trend is multi-pronged evaluation, combining controlled false-belief injection,
multi-turn pressure simulation, judge-based facet rating, and scalar indices, because no single
metric captures the full behavioral range. The progressive and regressive rates are defined only
for correctness-referenced instruments.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Measurement metrics for sycophancy quantification.}
\label{tab:metrics}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{11.4cm}l@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Definition} & \textbf{Source} \\
\midrule
Sycophancy rate & Share of responses agreeing with the user's (often wrong) position &
  \cite{fanous2025syceval} \\
Progressive rate & Share of capitulations landing on correct answers & \cite{fanous2025syceval} \\
Regressive rate & Share of capitulations landing on incorrect answers & \cite{fanous2025syceval} \\
Turn of Flip (ToF) & Turns until the model first changes stance under pressure &
  \cite{hong2025sycon} \\
Number of Flips & Stance changes across a conversation & \cite{hong2025sycon} \\
AEDI score & Continuous sensitivity of expressed support to user attitude & \cite{botas2026aedi} \\
Authority follow rate & Share of cases following an incorrect authority figure &
  \cite{celebi2025parrot} \\
Misleading resistance & Accuracy maintained under a user's false premise & \cite{zhang2025pressure} \\
Rubric score & Judge-rated facets (agreement, flattery, validation) & \cite{dubois2026askdont} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\section{Glossary}
\label{app:glossary}

\begin{description}[leftmargin=0pt,itemsep=2pt,parsep=0pt]
\item[Sycophancy.] The tendency of an LLM to prioritize user approval over epistemic accuracy,
  typically produced by preference-optimization dynamics that reward responses raters prefer in
  the short term.
\item[Propositional sycophancy.] Affirming or conceding to the user's expressed factual beliefs,
  including false ones; the harm is epistemic rather than relational.
\item[Social sycophancy.] Face-preserving behavior without necessary propositional error:
  flattery, unsolicited praise, avoidance of corrective feedback, affirmation of self-image.
\item[Progressive / regressive sycophancy.] Classification of capitulation by outcome: toward a
  correct answer (possibly appropriate updating) or toward an incorrect one (unconditionally
  harmful). Defined only when a correctness label exists (reference type R).
\item[Reference type.] What an instrument judges a response against: a correctness label (R), a
  counterfactual stance condition with evidence held fixed (C), a human or clinical norm (N), or
  the update the evidence warrants (E).
\item[Displacement.] Suppression of one form of sycophancy that leaves the influence in place in
  a form the evaluating instrument does not observe, such as an unverbalized cue.
\item[Reward hacking / specification gaming.] Maximizing a proxy reward in ways that diverge from
  the intended objective; sycophancy games the human-approval proxy.
\item[Proxy Compression Hypothesis.] Framework formalizing reward hacking as exploitation of the
  information lost when high-dimensional human objectives are compressed into a scalar reward.
\item[Warm persona effect.] Higher sycophancy in models fine-tuned for warm, empathetic personas,
  strongest when users express distress.
\item[Sycophantic anchors.] Sentences in a reasoning trace that causally commit the model to
  agreement before the final answer, detectable mid-inference.
\item[Citation-based sycophancy.] Disproportionate persuasion by rebuttals containing citations,
  even fabricated ones, because citation-bearing pushback resembles expert correction.
\item[Epistemic entrainment.] Hypothesized longitudinal erosion of a user's epistemic thresholds
  and self-correction capacity under chronic exposure to belief-confirming AI.
\end{description}

\section{Open Science}
\label{app:openscience}

All artifacts are publicly available on GitHub~\citep{trivedi2026sycophancyartifact}, at
\url{https://github.com/devharsh/sok-sycophancy-401-artifact}; release v1.0 matches this paper.
The repository contains (i) the search: the 523 frozen DataCite records, all
423 screening decisions with their quotes, the coding of the 44 instruments, the mitigation
grades with full-text evidence, and a script that recomputes every count in this paper;
(ii) the systematization: the coded corpus, benchmark, mitigation, metric, and coverage tables as
CSV files with a validator that fails if they drift from the tables in this paper; and (iii) the
figure sources (a Jupyter notebook and a draw.io diagram). No human-subject or proprietary data are
used. All references were checked against their primary records (publisher pages, proceedings
indexes, DataCite or arXiv records, or vendor sites) in August and October 2026.

\section{Ethical Considerations}
\label{app:ethics}

This work analyzes previously published studies and involves no new data collection, no human
subjects, and no deanonymization. Two points warrant statement. First, a threat model for eliciting
biased model output could be read as instructional; we judge the exposure risk low because every
elicitation effect cited is already published, because the phrasing effects involved are simple
and widely known, and because the defender-side value of naming the channels exceeds the marginal
information provided to adversaries. Second, the instruction-injection observation
(Section~\ref{sec:injection}) is presented as an illustrative incident, not as evidence; it
describes mechanism and consequences without identifying the document or reproducing the payload,
since such text can be placed defensively by authors, and the class is independently documented
\citep{lin2025hiddenprompts}. The proposed longitudinal study (Section~\ref{subsec:entrainment}) specifies debriefing,
referral pathways, and independent safety monitoring as preconditions.
